::ENG4_U05_L04_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The schedule is provisional and may change after the committee votes. What does provisional mean? {
~A. final and permanent.#Incorrect. The schedule may change, so it is not final.
=B. temporary or conditional.#Correct. The context says it may change after a later event.
~C. careless and unplanned.#Incorrect. The sentence does not imply carelessness.
~D. secret and unavailable.#Incorrect. Availability is not addressed.
}
::ENG4_U05_L04_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The word predictable comes from predict and the suffix -able. What does predictable mean? {
=A. able to be expected or forecast.#Correct. Predict means forecast, and -able means able to be.
~B. unable to be understood.#Incorrect. That would conflict with the word parts.
~C. already completed.#Incorrect. The word parts do not show completion.
~D. spoken in public.#Incorrect. The word parts do not refer to speech.
}
::ENG4_U05_L04_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The evidence was inconclusive, so the researcher delayed announcing a final claim. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. The evidence proved the claim completely.#Incorrect. Inconclusive means not settled.
~B. The researcher ignored all evidence.#Incorrect. The researcher responded to the evidence.
=C. The evidence did not settle the issue, so the researcher waited to make a final claim.#Correct. This preserves the meaning of inconclusive and delayed.
~D. The researcher announced the final claim immediately.#Incorrect. The passage says delayed.
}
::ENG4_U05_L04_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The report was concise rather than exhaustive. What word relationship helps determine concise? {
~A. Cause and effect.#Incorrect. The sentence contrasts two descriptions.
~B. Example and category.#Incorrect. No category list is provided.
~C. Definition and restatement.#Incorrect. The meaning is not directly restated.
=D. Contrast or antonym.#Correct. Rather than signals a contrast between concise and exhaustive.
}
::ENG4_U05_L04_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Reference entry: qualified means limited by conditions; officially trained; modified. Passage: The senator gave qualified support only if oversight was added. Which meaning fits? {
~A. officially trained.#Incorrect. The sentence is about support, not training.
=B. limited by conditions.#Correct. Only if oversight was added shows conditional support.
~C. physically repaired.#Incorrect. That meaning is not in the entry or context.
~D. unrelated to limits.#Incorrect. The phrase only if shows a limit.
}
